 so it's really  important to to to do this check but as  we talked about in point two that to not  do this check too often  i um you know  i was i was thinking uh about the fact  that for example one thing that i always  do i i know it might sound surprising to  you because assuming is a the resource  the one resource i used to  get a foundation in the language at the  beginning i never ever finish astronomy  a unit 17 around 70 maybe 75  i just tell myself this has become  boring so something that was super  exciting at the beginning you know even  the the most the simplest of dialogues  like hi my name is maria hi my name is  john it's exciting because you don't  know anything of the language but after  three months of doing this you know i  applied my biracial translation to it  then i tell myself this is boring and  when that moment happens then you have  to have to it's not an option you have  to move to something else because if i  had to continue with asthma for three  more months i would not continue  learning the language  so it's really important to have some  periodic checks i have my own system in  place so i know that after three months  or four months of doing asthma i move to  something else but keep that in mind  because having periodic checks of three  months to four months and changing the  material the way you do it when you do  it for how long it really makes a  difference i always like saying that the  way i learn language is when people ask  me about the method i have it's not a  method it's not one method but it's a  combination a series of methods that  evolve with time and it's important to  keep that in mind  and this goes a bit against human nature  because we have  that in human nature it's you need  closure you started that book you need  to finish it and that just it just  doesn't work well with with learning  languages if if you're not  making progress if you're not motivated  to continue with it anymore it's better  to stop here than to stop than to  continue a few more weeks and then lose  motivation completely so you need to  fight that urge for closure and just  just stop like even in the middle of it  i mean there are some perfectionists who  really want to finish  reading or going through the book and by  all means if you want to do that do that  but  as gabriel was pointing it out if you  feel like that book has become boring  and not effective then you should move  to  on to something else maybe you can just  quickly read the other parts without  having to go through and understand  everything but i think that you know a  few changes here and there are important  along the long long path of language  learning okay so we talked about getting  creating finding your own material we've  talked about systems focusing on systems  rather than goals  we've talked about changing these  language learning materials and it's all  great you know listening reading  exposing yourself to input and content  what about speaking what about um  polyglots what do they do to move from  input uh into the realm of output you  know what's been your experience have  you started learning a language from  scratch at the beginning and started  speaking it from day one have you waited  a little bit and how do you move from  you know doing a language learning  material input output listening and  reading to actually speaking and  producing the language  yeah so my my views evolved on that and  now i think that it's the best to  listen to a lot before starting to speak  and that's important because first of  all you need to understand how the  language works how the language sounds  because every new language has sounds  that you don't hear at the beginning but  the more you listen to it the more you  start pick up those sounds and if you  start speaking too soon then you'll  start speaking without those sounds  which means that you'll have bad  pronunciation you'll have a bad accent  and that's really difficult to correct  later down the line so it's really  important to listen to a lot to do a lot  of input comprehensible input  for as long as possible until you really  feel comfortable until you really  understand a lot until you have a  feeling for the structure for the  language and then start speaking and at  some point you'll have to start speaking  obviously  but if you already have a really strong  foundation that you built through input  then very often you start speaking  and you're surprised how many things you  can say you picked up so many phrases so  many chunks of the language and you just  start speaking and you just know things  that you didn't even know that you know  and it's it's just great because you  already start speaking from a from a  much better basis than you would if you  start speaking from day one  you have a better pronunciation and it's  just more fun and so that's why i think  it's really important to to listen to a  lot to really get a good foundation but  then at some point you need to get to  speaking and you just need to do it and  not just a few sentences here or there  but to really  look for language partners or look for  language teachers that you can spend  several hours per week speaking to to  really progress fast have you ever tried  to speak a language from day one without  listening too much  maybe a few words here and there um i'm  not sure  i'd have i i think  in the past i've started earlier than  i'm doing it now because you just you  just have the urge to start doing  something but now that i'm really  focusing on input first like really  doing comprehensible input as stephen  krashen uh always suggested suggested i  just it's just so much it's nicer you  feel more comfortable speaking and you  know  a lot of things that you're saying are  correct because you've heard native  speakers say it so many times and it's  just it's just stuck in your brain  i agree with you i generally agree with  you and this this speaking from day 