d and i think that this is a  technique that really helps me um  starting to speak the language without  the stress of having to speak because  speaking a language foreign language for  the first time is kind of stressful  i don't know if it's stressful for you  to speak a foreign language for the  first time but i guess  you know i guess it is yeah it's always  it's your brain is on overdrive it's you  grasp all words and and sentences that  you've heard before that you learned and  it's very stressful it takes a lot of  energy you know  gavel speaks six languages i speak 14  languages even people who have  experience in language learning  it's not true that  you  learn how to speak confidently for every  language every single time you start a  new language you go through the same  stuff you go through the insecurity of  speaking you're aware of that that makes  that's what makes a difference but the  truth is when you start using the  language for the first time it's always  a little bit stressful and your mind  goes blank and you think about stuff you  look at the other person's face you  think oh my god she's thinking or he is  thinking i'm a complete dumb dumb ass  you know  so anyway uh that's it for point number  four okay  now let's talk about one thing that has  really made a difference in my life and  my life as a person in my life as a  polyglot as a language learner and that  is creating circumstances right we tend  to think that language learning is about  hitting the book so to speak but i do  believe that the bulk of my skills i've  built them through by talking to people  by experiencing things in life right so  what's what's your take on that i think  that polyglots what politics make  do differently is that they create the  circumstances through which and in which  they can make their language learning  skills thrive yeah it's we talked about  this earlier that it's really important  to  be in touch with that language all the  time and and some people  that haven't learned languages before  find  it's a hard time for them to find the  people to speak to to find opportunities  and polyglots they just find  opportunities everywhere they they go to  international parties they they watch  international tv  for instance i always like to schedule  teachers  calls with teachers where i will have  that opportunity at the specific time  slot or traveling to that country but  really think where can you get in touch  with that language be listening to it or  being speaking to someone and this is  just what drives your language skills  forward really fast and it's basically  the immersion that we've talked about  already it's surrounding yourself with a  language all the time and it's really  important to find those opportunities  all the time and there's so many  opportunities in today's world that is  globalized that has internet with where  you find lots of content where you can  connect with everybody around the world  and that's just really important and  that's what also makes the language  learning process fun is having those  opportunities and not just always  looking at it in theory in books but  rather having the opportunity to use the  language in real life because how we  learn it in school is that  we learn the theory for years and years  and years and then at some point in the  very distant future you'll have the  opportunity to speak that language  fluently but most people  know that that's not how it works you  have to always have those opportunities  and you have to always look for those  opportunities to use that language the  opportunities are here  here  and now and around the corner it's up to  you to grab them and when people say for  example oh  it's true you guys live in europe yet  sure it's true that maybe in europe it's  a little bit easier than if you live say  in australia but the reality is that  with the internet and even in in your  town there are opportunities to find  people you know i'v